# APPL intake (read this before touching anything the user asked you to "APPL")

**APPL is the name of a pipeline (Agent Parallel Proof Loop). It is not the verb "apply" and not 「適用」.** 「〜を APPL して」, "APPL this", "use APPL for", "send this to APPL" all mean ONE thing: file the task so a separate coordinator session runs it through test author -> implementer -> reviewer -> publisher. "Add to the backlog", "queue", "later:", "idea:" (and 「バックログに積んで」) mean the same pipeline, later: the idea goes to the backlog inbox. 「適用して」 / "apply this" is NOT APPL; if you are unsure which the user meant, ask, do not act.

When asked to APPL something, do exactly this and nothing else:

1. Do NOT implement, edit files, create or reclaim worktrees, start Herdr workers, redeploy anything, or "coordinate" the task yourself. A session that does any of that has misread the instruction.
2. Run, from the top of the repository the user is talking about, the vendored parser with the user's sentence EXACTLY as written (trigger words, priority words and all; not restated, not translated, not escaped) on the one line between the quoted heredoc markers, so nothing in it is expanded or run:

   ```bash
   rel=".cursor/skills/isolated-session/scripts/appl-phrase.py"
   is_xyz() {
     case "$1" in
       *[!0-9.]*) return 1 ;;
       *.*.*.*) return 1 ;;
       *.*) ;;
       *) return 1 ;;
     esac
     local a b c saved_ifs
     saved_ifs=$IFS
     IFS=.
     read -r a b c <<< "$1"
     IFS=$saved_ifs
     [ -n "$a" ] && [ -n "$b" ] && [ -n "$c" ]
   }
   xyz_gt() {
     local a1 a2 a3 b1 b2 b3 saved_ifs
     saved_ifs=$IFS
     IFS=.
     read -r a1 a2 a3 <<< "$1"
     read -r b1 b2 b3 <<< "$2"
     IFS=$saved_ifs
     if [ "$a1" -gt "$b1" ]; then
       return 0
     fi
     if [ "$a1" -lt "$b1" ]; then
       return 1
     fi
     if [ "$a2" -gt "$b2" ]; then
       return 0
     fi
     if [ "$a2" -lt "$b2" ]; then
       return 1
     fi
     [ "$a3" -gt "$b3" ]
   }
   parser=""
   if [ -f "$rel" ]; then
     parser="$rel"
   else
     ver=""
     if [ -f appl.toml ]; then
       while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
         case "$line" in
           'version = "'*'"')
             prefix='version = "'
             ver=${line#"$prefix"}
             suffix='"'
             ver=${ver%"$suffix"}
             break
             ;;
         esac
       done < appl.toml
     fi
     is_xyz "$ver" || ver=""
     if [ -n "$ver" ] && [ -d "$HOME/.appl/$ver" ]; then
       parser="$HOME/.appl/$ver/$rel"
     elif [ -d "$HOME/.appl/latest" ]; then
       parser="$HOME/.appl/latest/$rel"
     else
       best=""
       if [ -d "$HOME/.appl" ]; then
         for dir in "$HOME/.appl"/*; do
           [ -d "$dir" ] || continue
           name="${dir##*/}"
           is_xyz "$name" || continue
           if [ -z "$best" ] || xyz_gt "$name" "$best"; then
             best="$name"
           fi
         done
       fi
       if [ -n "$best" ]; then
         parser="$HOME/.appl/$best/$rel"
       fi
     fi
   fi
   if [ -z "$parser" ]; then
     echo "appl-phrase: no install; run appl install" >&2
     exit 1
   fi
   APPL_FROM="cursor:<your model>" python3 "$parser" -- "$(cat <<'APPL_SENTENCE'
   <the user's sentence, verbatim, on one line>
   APPL_SENTENCE
   )"
   ```

   You no longer pass `--from "cursor:<your model>" --repo "$PWD"` to a filing script yourself: the parser takes the session from `APPL_FROM`, the repository from the directory it runs in, and owns the phrase table that decides intake-now or backlog, the priority and the rest. Never call a filing script directly. When .cursor/skills/isolated-session/scripts/appl-phrase.py is absent from the repository, resolve the parser from the install and run that parser with the repository as the working directory. The order is: the version named in the repository's appl.toml, under ~/.appl/<version>/; else ~/.appl/latest/ when that path exists, falling through when it is absent; else the newest ~/.appl/<v>/ directory, the greatest X.Y.Z. Refuse only when no install exists at all, and then the refusal names `appl install` and files nothing. Do not substitute your own implementation.
3. Tell the user its one line and stop. Exit 0: it names what was filed, the repository and the path; the result does not come back to this chat. A line ending in `?`: nothing was filed; ask the user that question, and hand their answer over the same way (answered "the backlog" to "now or backlog?": the same command with `--backlog` before `--`). Exit 3 with an `appl-add:` line (for example `coordinator pane not running`): the intake is filed but the coordinator was NOT woken; pass that line on unchanged. Any other `appl-phrase:` refusal: pass it on unchanged; nothing was filed.

If the user later asks "did APPL take it?", read the filed file's `status:` line; do not start work.
